//%% [instellingen] waarden=uit code=verborgen
//%% [markdown]
// # Notebooks: the Killer App for Sapl & the JMVM
//
// ### Pure, lazy, interactive documents, entirely in the browser
//
// **Jan Martin Jansen** · November 2026
//
// *This is not a slide program: it is a notebook. Every slide runs live in
// this browser tab (WebAssembly). Press **C** to see the code underneath,
// **Esc** for the ordinary notebook view, **P** to return to the slides.*
//%%
import "lib/list.spp" as L
import "lib/text.spp" as T
//%% [markdown]
// ## Who is waiting for another functional language?
//
// - A fast lazy VM (**JMVM**), a self-hosting compiler for **Sapl**, a type
//   checker, a REPL, a linker: nice, but *nobody* is waiting for another
//   compiler.
// - A language becomes interesting with a **killer app**: something people
//   want to use, where the language is the reason it works so well.
// - For Sapl and the JMVM that app is the **interactive document**: the
//   notebook.
//
// <div style="display: flex; gap: 20px; align-items: stretch; margin: 22px 0;">
//   <div style="flex: 1; padding: 16px 20px; border: 1px solid var(--border, #333); border-radius: 10px; background: rgba(56, 189, 248, 0.08);">
//     <div style="font-size: 19px; font-weight: 700; color: #38bdf8; margin-bottom: 6px;">The language</div>
//     <div style="font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.45;">Pure and lazy: no hidden state, definitions cost nothing until used, results are shared.</div>
//   </div>
//   <div style="flex: 1; padding: 16px 20px; border: 1px solid var(--border, #333); border-radius: 10px; background: rgba(16, 185, 129, 0.08);">
//     <div style="font-size: 19px; font-weight: 700; color: #10b981; margin-bottom: 6px;">The VM</div>
//     <div style="font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.45;">Small, fast, WebAssembly: compiler, linker and runtime all in the browser tab.</div>
//   </div>
//   <div style="flex: 1; padding: 16px 20px; border: 2px solid #c084fc; border-radius: 10px; background: rgba(192, 132, 252, 0.12);">
//     <div style="font-size: 19px; font-weight: 700; color: #c084fc; margin-bottom: 6px;">The notebook</div>
//     <div style="font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.45;">Instant, reproducible, private, interactive, with zero installation.</div>
//   </div>
// </div>
//%% [markdown]
// ## The problem with classic notebooks
//
// Jupyter with Python is the standard, and it has well-known flaws, all
// rooted in **mutable global state**:
//
// - **Hidden state.** Cells run in any order; a deleted or edited cell
//   leaves its objects behind in the kernel. Results are not reproducible.
// - **Re-computation.** Change a parameter upstream and the safe answer is
//   *"Restart Kernel & Run All"*.
// - **Eager by nature.** Loading a big dataset or an endless sequence
//   allocates everything at once.
// - **Heavy infrastructure.** A Python kernel lives in a container on a
//   server: 15–40 s to start on Binder or Colab, hosting costs, and your
//   data leaves your machine.
//
// Reactive notebooks (Pluto.jl, Observable, marimo) build dependency graphs,
// but they keep fighting against mutation. **In Sapl there is no mutation
// to fight.**
//%% [markdown]
// ## Pure + lazy = reactive for free
//
// - **No mutation, no invalidation conflicts.** A cell is an expression; its
//   result is a function of its inputs. Edit a definition and exactly its
//   dependents are recomputed.
// - **Laziness makes definitions free.** A cell may define a million-element
//   pipeline or an endless list: it costs nothing until another cell asks
//   for a piece of it.
// - **Automatic memoisation.** A top-level value (`=:`, a CAF) is evaluated
//   once; the VM overwrites the thunk with its value (graph reduction), and
//   every later cell reuses it.
//
// Below: an endless list of primes is *defined*, then two cells each take
// only what they need.
//%%
primes =: sieve (L.from 2)

sieve xs = case xs
  (Nil -> Nil)
  (Cons p rest -> Cons p (sieve (L.filter (\n -> n % p != 0) rest)))
//%%
L.take 12 primes
//%%
L.takeWhile (\p -> p < 300) (L.dropWhile (\p -> p < 200) primes)
//%% [markdown]
// ## Why the JMVM is the ideal engine for notebooks
//
// - **Thunk graph reduction.** Unevaluated expressions live on the heap as
//   small thunks; only what a cell (or widget) asks for is ever forced.
//   Endless streams need no pagination logic.
// - **A persistent heap.** Editing cell 10 does not throw away the work of
//   cells 1–9: evaluated CAFs stay evaluated.
// - **Isolation in a Web Worker.** The VM runs beside the page, not in it.
//   An endless loop never freezes the browser tab.
// - **Recoverable faults.** `try` catches `error`, failed pattern matches, a
//   full heap or stack; the cell shows the message, the session lives on.
// - **100% client side.** Compiler, linker, standard library and VM are
//   static WebAssembly and JavaScript: works offline, costs no servers, and
//   your data never leaves the browser.
//%%
square n = if (n < 0) (error "no negative numbers, please") (T.showInt (n * n))

recover msg = T.concat ["caught: ", msg]
//%%
[try (square 12) recover, try (square (0 - 3)) recover]
//%% [markdown]
// ## Fast: well inside one animation frame
//
// Measured in the WebSapl WebAssembly worker:
//%%
D.table ["what happens", "time", "notes"]
  [ ["edit one cell", "6–8 ms", "parse, compile, link, install and run; under the 16 ms frame budget"],
    ["edit a definition + its dependents", "19–25 ms", "recomputes exactly the dependent cells"],
    ["open a notebook (cold)", "122–250 ms", "including the standard library and the page"],
    ["unused definition cells", "0 ms", "lazy: never evaluated"],
    ["Python kernel in a container", "15–40 s", "Binder / Colab, for comparison"] ]
//%% [markdown]
// ## How a cell runs: compile, link, install, all in memory
//
// No files, no processes, no restarted VM. Every step is Sapl, except the
// final installation, which is one VM primitive.
//
// <div style="display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 8px; align-items: center; margin: 18px 0; font-size: 14px;">
//   <div style="padding: 10px 12px; border: 1px solid #38bdf8; border-radius: 8px;"><b>edit</b><br>a cell</div>➔
//   <div style="padding: 10px 12px; border: 1px solid #38bdf8; border-radius: 8px;"><b>:nb</b><br>message to the worker</div>➔
//   <div style="padding: 10px 12px; border: 1px solid #10b981; border-radius: 8px;"><b>saplcomp_module</b><br>chunk → .jvb bytes</div>➔
//   <div style="padding: 10px 12px; border: 1px solid #10b981; border-radius: 8px;"><b>LK.lkLink</b><br>linker in Sapl</div>➔
//   <div style="padding: 10px 12px; border: 1px solid #c084fc; border-radius: 8px;"><b>@install</b><br>hot-load into the VM</div>➔
//   <div style="padding: 10px 12px; border: 1px solid #c084fc; border-radius: 8px;"><b>applyDynamic</b><br>run it</div>➔
//   <div style="padding: 10px 12px; border: 1px solid #f59e0b; border-radius: 8px;"><b>render</b><br>value, table, picture</div>
// </div>
//
// - The **modular compiler** compiles one cell, not the whole program, and
//   knows the standard library from precompiled module headers.
// - The **linker** is written in Sapl: relocations, constructors and types
//   are merged into the running program.
// - **Values survive reloads**: `@@values` / `@@restore` store evaluated
//   values (even functions and unevaluated thunks) with the notebook.
// - The type checker reports problems per cell, before anything runs.
//%% [markdown]
// ## Interactive cells: the Elm architecture
//
// `D.interactive model update view`, or `D.animated ms model update view`
// for a tick every `ms` milliseconds. The model is ordinary Sapl data;
// `update` is a pure function from an event (click, key, tick, form input)
// and a model to the next model; `view` turns the model into a picture,
// table or form.
//
// A Lissajous figure: change the settings in the form while it turns.
//%%
import "lib/shape.spp" as Sh

::Colour = Red | Green | Blue

::Setting = { !fx : Int, !fy : Int, !colour : Colour, !line : Bool }

::Figure = { !setting : Setting, !phase : Float }

figStart = Figure (Setting 3 2 Blue True) 0.0

figStep ev f = case ev
  (D.Input field s -> Figure (Sh.input field s (setting f)) (phase f))
  (D.Tick t -> Figure (setting f) (phase f + 0.04))
  (_ -> f)

figView f = D.column [D.form (setting f), D.picture (lissajous (setting f) (phase f))]

colourNr k = case k (Red -> 12) (Green -> 10) (Blue -> 9)

lissajous i ph =
  let pts = [ G.Pt (0.9 * sin (itof (fx i) * t + ph)) (0.9 * sin (itof (fy i) * t)) | t <- L.map (\k -> itof k * 0.0315) (L.range 0 200) ] in
  if (line i) [G.GraphPolyLine (colourNr (colour i)) pts]
    (L.map (dot (colourNr (colour i))) pts)

dot c p = case p (G.Pt x y -> G.GraphDisc c (G.Pt (x - 0.012) (y - 0.012)) (G.Pt (x + 0.012) (y + 0.012)))
//%%
D.animated 40 figStart figStep figView
//%% [markdown]
// ## A Hilbert curve, drawn line by line
//
// From an old Amanda program: each level is four rotated and shifted copies
// of the previous one, glued end to start (`tcat`). The routes are CAFs:
// computed once, then shared by every frame. Choose the `level` and the
// `speed` in the form; click to restart, press a key to pause.
//%%
::HilbSetting = { !level : Int, !speed : Int }

::Hilb = { !hSetting : HilbSetting, !drawn : Int, !paused : Bool }

hilbStart = Hilb (HilbSetting 4 2) 2 False

clamp lo hi n = if (n < lo) lo (if (n > hi) hi n)

mirrorX p = case p (G.Pt x y -> G.Pt (0.0 - x) y)
mirrorY p = case p (G.Pt x y -> G.Pt x (0.0 - y))
rot90 p = case p (G.Pt x y -> G.Pt (0.0 - y) x)
shift dx dy p = case p (G.Pt x y -> G.Pt (x + dx) (y + dy))

tcat xs ys = case (L.last xs) (G.Pt lx ly -> L.append xs (L.map (shift lx ly) ys))

hilbLevel v =
  tcat (tcat (tcat
    (L.map (\p -> mirrorX (rot90 p)) v)
    (L.map (shift 1.0 0.0) v))
    (L.map (shift 0.0 1.0) v))
    (L.map (\p -> shift (0.0 - 1.0) 0.0 (mirrorY (rot90 p))) v)

hilb n = if (n <= 0) [G.Pt 0.0 0.0] (hilbLevel (hilb (n - 1)))

ptX p = case p (G.Pt x y -> x)
ptY p = case p (G.Pt x y -> y)

fit pts =
  let xs = L.map ptX pts, ys = L.map ptY pts,
      x0 = L.minimum xs, x1 = L.maximum xs,
      y0 = L.minimum ys, y1 = L.maximum ys,
      s = 1.6 / (if (x1 - x0 > y1 - y0) (x1 - x0) (y1 - y0)),
      mx = (x0 + x1) * 0.5, my = (y0 + y1) * 0.5 in
  [G.Pt ((ptX p - mx) * s) ((ptY p - my) * s) | p <- pts]

hilbRoutes =: [ fit (hilb k) | k <- [1 .. 6] ]

hilbTotal n = L.foldl (\a b -> a * 4) 1 (L.range 1 n)

hilbStep ev h = case ev
  (D.Input field s ->
     let new = Sh.input field s (hSetting h) in
     Hilb new (if (clamp 1 6 (level new) != clamp 1 6 (level (hSetting h))) 2 (drawn h)) (paused h))
  (D.Click x y -> Hilb (hSetting h) 2 (paused h))
  (D.Key k -> Hilb (hSetting h) (drawn h) (if (paused h) False True))
  (D.Tick t ->
     let i = hSetting h, n = clamp 1 6 (level i), step = clamp 1 256 (speed i) in
     if (paused h) h
       (Hilb i (if (drawn h >= hilbTotal n + 20 * step) 2 (drawn h + step)) False))
  (_ -> h)

hilbView h =
  let i = hSetting h,
      n = clamp 1 6 (level i),
      part = L.take (clamp 1 (hilbTotal n) (drawn h)) (L.nth (n - 1) hilbRoutes) in
  D.column
    [D.form i,
     D.picture [G.GraphPolyLine 11 part, dot 12 (L.last part)]]
//%%
D.animated 40 hilbStart hilbStep hilbView
//%% [markdown]
// ## The sieve of Eratosthenes on 10,000 dots
//
// A 100 × 100 grid: one dot for every number from 1 to 10,000. All dots
// (except 1) start **green**. Each step takes the next prime `p` (yellow)
// and turns its multiples **grey** (the newest ones light grey). After the
// primes below 100 nothing changes any more: the green dots that are left
// are exactly the 1,229 primes. Then it starts again. Press a key to pause,
// click to restart. The view here is one SVG string instead of a list of
// shapes: a view can be any display.
//%%
gridSize = 100

::Dot = Dot !num !factor !seg

smallestFactor n d = if (d * d > n) 0 (if (n % d == 0) d (smallestFactor n (d + 1)))

// One dot per number; `seg` is its piece of an SVG path (a round dot of
// length 0), computed once and shared by every frame.
dots =: [ makeDot n | n <- L.range 1 (gridSize * gridSize) ]

makeDot n =
  let k = n - 1 in
  Dot n (if (n == 1) 1 (smallestFactor n 2))
      (T.concat ["M", T.showInt (2 * (k % gridSize) + 1), " ", T.showInt (2 * (k / gridSize) + 1), "h0"])

smallPrimes =: L.filter (\p -> smallestFactor p 2 == 0) (L.range 2 gridSize)

::Sieve = { !stage : Int, !running : Bool }

sieveStart = Sieve 0 True

stages =: L.length smallPrimes + 8

sieveStep ev s = case ev
  (D.Tick t -> if (running s) (Sieve ((stage s + 1) % (stages + 1)) True) s)
  (D.Key k -> Sieve (stage s) (if (running s) False True))
  (D.Click x y -> Sieve 0 True)
  (_ -> s)

currentPrime s =
  if (stage s == 0) 1
    (if (stage s > L.length smallPrimes) (gridSize + 1) (L.nth (stage s - 1) smallPrimes))

// 0 = green (not crossed out), 1 = grey, 2 = light grey (crossed out by
// the current prime), 3 = yellow (the current prime)
dotState p d = case d
  (Dot n f seg ->
     if (n == 1) 1
       (if (n == p) 3
         (if (f == 0) 0
           (if (f == p) 2 (if (f < p) 1 0)))))

dotSeg d = case d (Dot n f seg -> seg)

dotPath p state colour =
  T.concat ["<path stroke='", colour, "' stroke-width='1.45' stroke-linecap='round' fill='none' d='",
            T.concat (L.map dotSeg (L.filter (\d -> dotState p d == state) dots)), "'/>"]

sieveView s =
  let p = currentPrime s, w = T.showInt (2 * gridSize) in
  D.markdown (T.concat
    ["<div><svg viewBox='0 0 ", w, " ", w, "' style='display:block;width:min(560px,90vw,60vh);height:auto;background:#0a0f1d;border-radius:8px'>",
     dotPath p 0 "#22c55e", dotPath p 1 "#4b5563", dotPath p 2 "#9ca3af", dotPath p 3 "#facc15",
     "</svg></div>"])
//%%
D.animated 400 sieveStart sieveStep sieveView
//%% [markdown]
// ## Beyond Sapl: Haskell lite in the same notebook
//
// A notebook cell can also be **Haskell** (label `hs`). *Haskell lite* is a
// Haskell front end written in Sapl+: type classes, `deriving`, records,
// `where`, list comprehensions. Overloaded functions are specialised per
// type (no dictionaries at run time). Translating and compiling a cell takes
// about 0.1 s, and it runs in the browser as well, on the same JMVM.
//%% [hs]
// data Shape = Circle Double | Rect Double Double
//   deriving Show
//
// area :: Shape -> Double
// area (Circle r) = 3.14159 * r * r
// area (Rect w h) = w * h
//
// hsPrimes :: [Int]
// hsPrimes = sieve [2 ..]
//   where sieve (p : xs) = p : sieve [x | x <- xs, x `mod` p /= 0]
//%% [hs]
// (take 10 hsPrimes, map area [Circle 1.0, Rect 2.0 3.5])
//%% [markdown]
// In the Workbench, whatever Haskell lite does not handle yet goes through
// the MicroHs front end, which also runs on the JMVM. One notebook, one VM,
// several languages.
//%% [markdown]
// ## Why notebooks are the killer app
//
// | what users want from a notebook | what Sapl + JMVM bring |
// |---|---|
// | reproducible results | purity: no hidden state, ever |
// | instant feedback | 6–8 ms per edited cell, in memory |
// | big or endless data | laziness: only what is shown is computed |
// | no installation, no servers | everything is WebAssembly in the tab |
// | privacy | data never leaves the browser |
// | interaction and pictures | Elm-style cells, forms from types, graphics |
// | more than one language | Sapl, Sapl+, Haskell lite, own languages (`:lang`) |
//
// **Use cases:** teaching (courses with live code), exploring data,
// modelling (e.g. C2Sketch task models), interactive papers, and
// presentations like this one.
//
// *A lazy functional VM is a curiosity. A lazy functional VM that makes
// documents come alive is a product.*
